# A named function whose interpolation starts with one of its own parameters is
# the shape the string-builder analysis licenses to write through: `text_build
# (n - 1) "{s}x"` reads `s` to make the new string and mentions it nowhere
# else, so by the time the join runs `s` is finished and the join can extend
# its buffer instead of allocating a fresh header.
#
# That licence rests on every caller handing the value over, which the analysis
# checks by counting mentions. A call inside a lambda breaks the count: `go`
# mentions `prefix` once and `list/map` hands it to `kept` once per element, so
# three joins all held the one buffer and the answer read ["abc" "abc" "abc"] —
# each pair aliasing a string the next call had already appended to. The oracle
# said ["a" "b" "c"] throughout, which is how this was found: the perf-history
# rows had been carrying counter names appended into one key for months.
#
# So the capture is what the fixture pins, not the lambda. `built` below is
# called inside a lambda too and keeps its licence, because the argument it
# hands over is made there rather than caught from outside.
import "std/io"
import "std/list"

fn kept prefix n
  "{prefix}{n}"

fn go prefix
  list/to_list (list/map ["a" "b" "c"] (n -> kept prefix n))

fn built n acc
  more n acc (n < 1)

fn more _ acc true
  acc

fn more n acc false
  built (n - 1) (push acc n)

# A per-element lambda nested INSIDE a once-only continuation: both halves of
# the rule at once. The map's callback still gets the capture check even though
# the continuation around it does not, and this is the shape a future change is
# most likely to break, because getting either half alone looks like it works —
# the narrow rule alone put the miscompilation back, and the broad rule alone
# cost pendbench its in-place push and two per cent of its instructions.
fn nested p
  print "{go p}"

fn shown p
  print "{go p}"

pub play =
  shown ""
  >> shown "-"
  >> io/write "" . (_ -> nested "+")
  >> print "{length (built 4 [])}"
